00:00:05.590 --> 00:00:06.230 align:start position:0%
european union home affairs ministers  will discuss  this week better ways to prevent  accidents in the mediterranean sea  as would-be asylum seekers head for  europe's shores  in poorly maintained boats the ministers  will examine what steps they can take in  the short term  to help avoid tragedies like the one off  the italian island of lampedusa  debate will be based on a report by the  task force for the mediterranean  which has identified priority actions  that should be taken  the tragedies at sea will be a focus  when european leaders meet in brussels  in june  ministers will also discuss the problem  of foreign fighters from europe  crossing the border into syria to fight  and the security threat they could pose  in the eu when they return  the flow of foreign fighters traveling  to syria from the eu and other countries  has not diminished  on the contrary numbers seem to be on  the rise  the ministers will examine proposals to  dissuade them from leaving and more  efficient use of eu instruments for  information exchange and judicial  cooperation  they'll also look at how the eu can  boost its engagement with other  countries like syria's neighbors  through the exchange of best practices  and capacity building assistance  data protection is a key issue for  justice ministers  they'll discuss new ways to better  protect personal information  notably the so-called one-stop shop  principle  the council will also address the issue  of fully applying provisions of the  schengen passport-free  area in bulgaria and romania
 

